/* ============================================================================
   kaprekar-steps.epher -- steps to 6174 for a four-digit number

   Kaprekar's routine sorts the four digits of n into descending and
   ascending order, subtracts the smaller from the larger, and repeats;
   every four-digit number with at least two distinct digits reaches the
   constant 6174 in at most 7 steps (Kaprekar, 1949). The script sorts
   with the list builtin and subtracts with a packed while loop (guide
   1.30). The check: 3524 sorts to 5432 and 2345, and 5432 - 2345 = 3087,
   then 8730 - 0378 = 8352, then 8532 - 2358 = 6174: three steps.

   Source: D. R. Kaprekar, "An Interesting Property of the Number 6174",
   Scripta Mathematica 15 (1949); see also Martin Gardner, "The Magic of
   6174", in "Penrose Tiles to Trapdoor Ciphers". Results are exact.

   Run it from a terminal:
     Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/kaprekar-steps.epher
     Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\number-theory\integer-sequences\kaprekar-steps.epher"
     macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/number-theory/integer-sequences/kaprekar-steps.epher
   In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
   Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter

   The one knob to edit is `n` below: a four-digit number with at least
   two distinct digits (repdigits like 1111 get stuck at 0).
   ============================================================================ */

// ---- the knob -----------------------------------------------------------------
const n = 3524

/* ---- the digit-sorting helpers --------------------------------------------------
   The four digits come from the fixed places (thousands down to units),
   so a value like 3087 keeps its leading zero as the digit 0. sort puts
   them in ascending order; asc4 and desc4 read the sorted list out. */
def d4(n) = sort({mod(floor(n / 1000), 10), mod(floor(n / 100), 10), mod(floor(n / 10), 10), mod(n, 10)})
def asc4(n) = d4(n)[1] * 1000 + d4(n)[2] * 100 + d4(n)[3] * 10 + d4(n)[4]
def desc4(n) = d4(n)[4] * 1000 + d4(n)[3] * 100 + d4(n)[2] * 10 + d4(n)[1]

// ---- the first subtraction --------------------------------------------------------
print("3524 sorted:", asc4(3524), "and", desc4(3524))
print("5432 - 2345:", desc4(3524) - asc4(3524))      // 3087, the next value

// ---- the routine ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// v = {current value, steps}; subtract until the constant 6174 appears.
v = {n, 0}
while v[1] != 6174 do v = {desc4(v[1]) - asc4(v[1]), v[2] + 1}
print("6174 reached in", v[2], "steps")             // 3 for 3524

// ---- the fixed point ------------------------------------------------------------------
// 6174 sorts to 7641 and 1467, and 7641 - 1467 is 6174 again.
print("7641 - 1467:", desc4(6174) - asc4(6174))
/* ---- expected output ----
= 3524
= 3524 sorted: 2345 and 5432
= 5432 - 2345: 3087
= {3524, 0}
= 6174 reached in 3 steps
= 7641 - 1467: 6174
*/
